# An evidence-first ACCOUNTABILITY PROFILE of one candidate for one office — a
# substance-over-spin product that weights the DOCUMENTED RECORD (votes, offices and jobs
# actually held, money raised and given, court/agency records, controversies) ABOVE the
# campaign rhetoric, and explicitly contrasts the two. Fill {{candidate}} and {{office}}
# per run. The five gather angles bake in the accuracy + fairness discipline the research
# threads settled on (verify an absence before asserting it; label a legal matter by its
# real status; date and cite every claim to a primary source). See
# docs/plans/REPORT_PRESET_PLAN.md.
name: candidate_profile
description: Evidence-first accountability profile of one candidate — voting/legislative record, career & offices held, finances & funding, positions over time, and controversies/legal record, with campaign rhetoric checked against the record.
output_kind: report
sources: web

question: "Candidate accountability profile — {{candidate}} for {{office}}"

objective: |
  Produce an evidence-first ACCOUNTABILITY PROFILE of {{candidate}}, running for {{office}}:
  a substance-over-spin comparison document that weights the candidate's DOCUMENTED RECORD —
  votes, offices and jobs actually held, money raised and given, court and agency records —
  ABOVE their campaign rhetoric. Treat current campaign messaging as CLAIMS to be checked
  against that record, never as the substance itself.

  Cross-cutting rules (apply to every section):
  - Separate documented ACTION from CHARACTERIZATION. Use documenting verbs for the record
    ("voted for", "sponsored", "filed", "founded", "received $X from", "was convicted of")
    and attribute everything else ("the campaign says", "critics allege", "a fact-checker
    rated"). Never let a characterization wear the voice of a fact.
  - Date and source every claim; prefer a primary source (a roll-call vote, an FEC filing, a
    court docket, an official bio) over an aggregator's summary of it. Label a computed score
    (an ideology rating, a net-worth estimate) as that source's analysis, not a fact.
  - Neutral, quantified language ("missed 34% of votes in 2025", not "frequently absent"); no
    endorsement and no good/bad verdict — present the documented record and let the comparison
    speak. Keep the shape identical for every candidate so profiles line up side by side.
  - VERIFY AN ABSENCE BEFORE ASSERTING IT. Never claim "has no record / no committee / never
    did X" unless a researcher actually checked the authoritative source and found nothing.
    Where a section has no supporting findings, KEEP its heading and write the single line
    "Not established — no supporting source found as of the run date."
  - USE EVERY NAME THE RECORD REVEALS. Establish the candidate's full legal name AND any prior,
    maiden, married, or alternate names EARLY. Run `public_records(name, sources=["identity"])`
    (Wikidata) FIRST to harvest the canonical name and every alias/aka/maiden/former name it
    records; also mine the FEC committee's treasurer/candidate field, an official bio, news, and
    court records, which frequently expose one. Treat EACH name as a REQUIRED search key: re-run
    `public_records` under EVERY variant — its `license` source (for a physician/clinician:
    license number/state/specialty + other_names), `court` source (CourtListener), and
    `federal_register` source (agency debarments/enforcement) — before any "no record" statement.
    A profile that
    surfaces an alias in one section (e.g. a committee treasurer named differently from the ballot
    name) but asserts "no license / no record" in another section WITHOUT having searched that
    alias is DEFECTIVE — reconcile the two and search the alias before writing. A professional
    license, disciplinary action, or criminal case is very often filed under a prior/maiden name,
    not the ballot name.
  - AN EMPTY PORTAL IS NOT PROOF OF ABSENCE. A government search app (a state license lookup, a
    court-docket search) that returns nothing readable to the fetch is NOT evidence the record
    does not exist — a dynamic portal often cannot be read directly. Record such a result as
    "not verifiable via <source> as of the run date", try an alternate path (the portal's
    result/detail URL or search endpoint, a cached copy, an official disciplinary-action index,
    or news coverage of the record) before concluding anything, and NEVER upgrade "the search
    returned nothing" into "no record exists".
  - NON-INCUMBENT: a first-time candidate has no voting record — say so plainly under "Voting
    & Legislative Record"; never fabricate or infer votes. Run the record-vs-rhetoric contrast
    against their occupations, business conduct, donation history, and prior public statements
    instead.

  Section fill spec (use each heading verbatim, in order):
  - Summary: name, party, office sought, incumbency, and a two-to-three-sentence sourced
    bottom line on who they are BY RECORD.
  - Career & Offices Held: a dated timeline of the jobs, businesses, and public offices the
    candidate has actually held; verify résumé and business claims against records and note
    any a public record cannot confirm.
  - Voting & Legislative Record: key recorded votes, sponsored or co-sponsored bills, and
    committee actions, each dated and cited (or the non-incumbent note above). Flag any vote
    that conflicts with a position they now campaign on.
  - Positions Over Time: the candidate's stated positions on the major issues across time,
    each dated; name any documented SHIFT with both endpoints sourced and the candidate's own
    explanation of the change if they gave one.
  - Finances & Funding: money raised (committee name/ID, total receipts, cash on hand, top
    donors and industries, PAC vs. individual vs. self-funding), money the candidate has
    personally GIVEN (their own donation history — a revealed preference), personal financial
    disclosure if a federal candidate — all as the FEC and the records report them.
  - Endorsements & Backing: notable endorsements the candidate has RECEIVED — from
    officeholders and party figures (a president, governor, senator, or party leader),
    organizations, unions, PACs, or newspaper editorial boards — each attributed to who gave
    it and dated, with a source; note what the endorsement signals about their standing in the
    party/race. Say plainly if no notable endorsement is found.
  - Controversies & Legal Record: see the fairness rules below.
  - Record vs. Rhetoric: for each major current campaign claim or promise, set it beside the
    documented record and give ONE dated, sourced verdict, written as one of these labels:
    "MATCH" (the record confirms it), "CONTRADICTION" (the record shows the opposite action,
    vote, donation, or statement), or "NO RECORD" (the record is silent as of the run date).
    The documented side always outweighs the rhetorical one.
  - Assessment: a brief, sourced read of the CONSISTENCY between the candidate's record and
    their rhetoric — not their odds of winning.

  Controversies & Legal Record — fairness is MANDATORY (this is an accountability record, not
  a rumor mill). Every item MUST carry: (1) its date(s); (2) a primary-document source; (3) a
  precise STATUS LABEL — one of allegation / lawsuit filed / charge or indictment / settlement
  without admission / consent decree / conviction or adjudicated finding / dismissal /
  acquittal / overturned-or-vacated; (4) how it was resolved (or "ongoing"); and (5) the
  candidate's response, or "did not respond". An allegation or charge is NOT a finding of
  guilt; a settlement without admission is not an admission; always report a dismissal,
  acquittal, or reversal alongside the original matter. EXCLUDE rumor, innuendo, anonymous-only
  claims, sealed/expunged/juvenile records, and purely private matters with no bearing on
  public office. Where a category was checked and nothing qualifying was found, say so.

sections:
  - "Summary"
  - "Career & Offices Held"
  - "Voting & Legislative Record"
  - "Positions Over Time"
  - "Finances & Funding"
  - "Endorsements & Backing"
  - "Controversies & Legal Record"
  - "Record vs. Rhetoric"
  - "Assessment"

angles:
  - title: "Voting & legislative record"
    brief: >-
      Document {{candidate}}'s VOTING and LEGISLATIVE record relevant to {{office}} from
      NON-CAMPAIGN authorities. If they hold or have held office, pull their key recorded
      votes, sponsored/co-sponsored bills, and committee actions WITH DATES from sources like
      Ballotpedia's "key votes", GovTrack, Vote Smart (votesmart.org), Congress.gov, or the
      state legislature's site (LegiScan / Open States). Cite the specific vote or bill, not
      just the aggregator's summary. Flag any vote that conflicts with a position the
      candidate now campaigns on. NON-INCUMBENT: if the candidate has held NO legislative
      office, say so plainly and do NOT fabricate or infer a voting record — note the absence
      and move on (their prior statements and offices are covered by other angles). Report only
      documented actions, each with a source and date; label any GovTrack/Vote Smart computed
      score as that source's analysis, not fact.
  - title: "Career, occupations & business record"
    brief: >-
      Verify {{candidate}}'s stated career, occupations, and offices held for {{office}}, and
      surface documented business conduct. Confirm claimed roles, employers, companies, and
      dates against authoritative sources (Ballotpedia, an official bio, SEC EDGAR if they led
      a public company, a state business registry). Where they claim a business record ("built
      a successful company", "created jobs"), state whether public records can confirm it.
      Surface documented business conduct through FREE court records — CourtListener / RECAP
      (courtlistener.com) — for major litigation, bankruptcies, or liens, each labeled by its
      outcome (settled / dismissed / judgment). Report offices held with years. Document every
      claim with a source and date, and explicitly note any résumé claim public records cannot
      confirm.
  - title: "Funding, endorsements & backing"
    brief: >-
      Research who BACKS {{candidate}} for {{office}} — financially and politically. (a) MONEY
      IN: search the FEC (fec.gov) by name for their principal committee — report committee
      name + ID, total receipts, cash on hand, individual vs. PAC vs. self-funding, and top
      donors/industries (cross-check OpenSecrets for federal money, FollowTheMoney for state).
      The committee's treasurer/candidate name field often reveals the candidate's full LEGAL or
      PRIOR name (e.g. a maiden name that differs from the ballot name) — capture it verbatim and
      flag it explicitly as a disambiguation key the licensing/court/disciplinary checks must also
      search under, since a license or case is often filed under that name.
      (b) MONEY GIVEN (revealed preferences): search FEC individual contributions and the
      OpenSecrets donor lookup for donations {{candidate}} has personally MADE — who they have
      funded is a documented signal of alignment; flag any that contradict their current
      messaging. (c) Personal financial disclosure if a federal candidate (House Clerk / Senate
      eFD). (d) ENDORSEMENTS RECEIVED: find notable endorsements the candidate has been given —
      by officeholders and party figures (a president, governor, senator, party leader),
      organizations, unions, PACs, or newspaper editorial boards — each attributed to WHO gave
      it and dated, with a source (a campaign endorsements page, news coverage, or the
      endorser's own statement); note what each signals about their standing in the party/race.
      Report FEC figures as the FEC reports them, dated; a committee is often under the
      candidate's own name (e.g. a "NAME FOR SENATE") — do not conclude none exists until you
      have actually searched the FEC, and if none is found say "no FEC committee found under
      this name as of <date>". If no notable endorsement is found, say so plainly.
  - title: "Positions over time & rhetoric"
    brief: >-
      Build a DATED trail of {{candidate}}'s stated positions on the major issues for
      {{office}} (economy, healthcare, immigration, education, energy, and any signature
      issues), from the earliest available up to now, AND capture their CURRENT campaign
      messaging. For the PAST: prior campaign sites via the Wayback Machine (web.archive.org),
      past interviews, a Vote Smart Political Courage Test, Ballotpedia survey answers, dated
      statements. For the PRESENT: the current campaign "issues" page, press releases, and
      prominent social posts, each dated. Where a position appears to have CHANGED, capture
      BOTH endpoints with dates and sources, plus the candidate's own explanation if they gave
      one. Frame the current campaign messaging as CLAIMS to be checked against the record —
      not as established fact. A change is only a "shift" if both endpoints are documented.
  - title: "Controversies & legal record"
    brief: >-
      Compile a rigorously sourced, FAIRLY LABELED record of {{candidate}}'s controversies,
      legal matters, and detrimental public record for the {{office}} race — an accountability
      record, NOT a rumor mill. FIRST establish the candidate's full legal name and EVERY prior,
      maiden, married, or alternate name — run `public_records(name, sources=["identity"])` (Wikidata)
      to harvest the aliases, and also mine the FEC committee's treasurer/candidate field, the
      candidate's own bio, "formerly known as" mentions, and any court or license record — because a
      professional license, disciplinary action, or criminal case is frequently filed under a PRIOR or
      MAIDEN name rather than the ballot name. Then run EVERY category search below under EACH name
      variant, not just the ballot name, using `public_records` — its `license` source (NPPES — a
      clinician's license number/state/specialty and any other_names; NOTE it carries no discipline, so
      take the license + state to the state board), `court` source (CourtListener), and
      `federal_register` source (agency debarments/enforcement).
      Reject any record you cannot confirm belongs to this exact person (common-name mix-ups are the
      top error), but do NOT assert "no license / no record" for a name you never actually searched. If a state license or court portal returns
      nothing readable, treat it as "not verifiable via that source", try the portal's result/detail
      endpoint or news coverage of the action, and do not conclude the record is absent. Sweep
      accessible PRIMARY sources: free federal
      court records (CourtListener / RECAP, courtlistener.com), FEC enforcement (MUR case
      files), SEC litigation and DOJ press releases, state bar / medical / professional license
      lookups, and congressional or state ethics findings (the GovTrack misconduct database for
      incumbents). Use investigative journalism only as a LEAD to verify against the underlying
      record — never cite an anonymous or partisan-blog claim as fact. Run these categories:
      criminal; civil litigation; financial (bankruptcy, tax, fraud, business failure); ethics
      & campaign-finance; professional/regulatory discipline; labor & consumer; misuse of
      office (for officeholders); and documented scandalous prior statements (with full context,
      date, and original source). For EVERY item record: the date(s); a PRIMARY-DOCUMENT source;
      a precise STATUS LABEL (allegation / lawsuit filed / charge or indictment / settlement
      without admission / consent decree / conviction or adjudicated finding / dismissal /
      acquittal / overturned); the DISPOSITION (how it ended, or "ongoing"); and the
      candidate's RESPONSE or denial if any. An allegation or charge is NOT guilt; a settlement
      without admission is not an admission; always report a dismissal, acquittal, or reversal
      alongside the accusation. EXCLUDE rumor, innuendo, anonymous-only claims, sealed/expunged/
      juvenile records, and purely private matters with no bearing on public office. Where a
      category is checked and nothing qualifying is found, say "checked, nothing found".
